WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.600
Hello and welcome to this AI Coffee Break!

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.560
Recently, we have seen how Transformers&nbsp;
are being applied not only on text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.160
but on images or even on both combined!

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.000
How is it possible?

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.240
Aren’t images and text so different? We read text&nbsp;
from left to right and images are something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.880
we perceive at once.
Or do we?

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.520
In this video, we will explain why images&nbsp;
and text can be similar in the “eyes”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.520
a Transformer neural network and point out&nbsp;
what really makes images and text different;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.480
difference that could make&nbsp;
the Transformer stumble.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.200
On the representation level: What is text?

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.960
Text is a sequence of characters or words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.520
And it’s a sequence, because we read&nbsp;
from left to right (in English).

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.200
But neural networks do not work&nbsp;
directly with characters nor with text.&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.600
Neural networks work with points&nbsp;
determined by coordinates, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.080
numbers in high dimensional spaces.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.440
Neural networks are great at squeezing,&nbsp;
stretching, and bending the input space so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.320
the similar data points get close together and are&nbsp;
easy to discriminate from dissimilar data points.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:27.040
So the Transformers working with numbers, request&nbsp;
that characters or words are encoded into vectors.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.200
These vectors are ordered, preserving the&nbsp;
sequence structure of the text. There are neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.680
architectures, like the LSTM, where this order&nbsp;
is captured intrinsically by the architecture:

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.680
The LSTM does look at every&nbsp;
word vector one after the other.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.520
But the Transformer works on sets,&nbsp;
looks at everything in parallel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.840
so for the Transformer to know this order,&nbsp;
positional embeddings are added to the vectors.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.720
The vectors can be initialized with&nbsp;
numbers where words belonging together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.560
are already together, or they can be&nbsp;
one-hot encodings or token identifiers.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.240
It is always up to the loss-constrained&nbsp;
neural network to figure out a better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.760
inner representation where words belonging&nbsp;
together are shifted closer to each other.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.840
And that was it for the text,&nbsp;
the rest is Transformer magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.240
check out our video explaining how&nbsp;
the Transformer works linked below!

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.400
Then what are images? Grayscale images&nbsp;
are a matrix of numbers. A high number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.960
corresponds to a high light intensity in&nbsp;
that region. A low number is a darker spot.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.600
For color images we just have a&nbsp;
stack of three of these matrices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.720
each corresponding to the&nbsp;
red, blue and green channel.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.440
A high number in the red matrix corresponds&nbsp;
to a high intensity of red light.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.640
But once the image is stored, the computer has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.200
these three matrices and the color&nbsp;
is not exactly there anymore.&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.640
The computer would not notice if we would just&nbsp;
exchange the numbers between these matrices.&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:10.080
Colour occurs again in your monitor:&nbsp;
if there is a high value in the matrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.240
labelled with red, it will emit light with&nbsp;
the certain light frequency that we call red.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.560
So let’s look at the word vectors again:&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:25.040
If we take their sequence and put them one&nbsp;
under the other, we have… an image! Crazy right?&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.160
Let’s undo this again and do&nbsp;
something different instead:

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.600
Take the image and treat&nbsp;
it like a sequence of rows.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.880
And this idea is not that crazy, it is exactly how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:41.760
cathode-ray tube screens in old TVs and old&nbsp;
monitors treated this matrix as a sequence by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.120
“drawing” the image from left&nbsp;
to right and from top to bottom!

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.760
In this way, the representation&nbsp;
of text and images is the same:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.880
the text is a sequence of word vectors,&nbsp;
the image is a sequence of image vectors.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.280
We can add positional embeddings and&nbsp;
a Transformer is ready to process text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.720
or images individually or both at the&nbsp;
same time for multimodal integration.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.960
And the Transformer can process them quite&nbsp;
efficiently because computations are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.240
parallel on this set of vectors, which&nbsp;
is not really a sequence, it's a set.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.680
The sequentiality is quite artificially&nbsp;
encoded in these positional embeddings.&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.760
In this way Transformers kind of butcher the&nbsp;
way we humans thought about text and images.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:32.400
We are comfortable with the LSTMs inductive&nbsp;
bias of looking at one word after the other.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.000
So why is the Transformer looking&nbsp;
at everything at the same time?

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.880
Well, I do not know about you, but&nbsp;
for me, when I am reading out loud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.680
I have to peek much further away into&nbsp;
the sequence to get the right intonation.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.240
I also peek into the past by&nbsp;
short-term memorization which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.200
honestly better than the memory of the LSTM.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.440
We are also comfortable with&nbsp;
CNNs that do a very similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.880
kind of processing as what happens in our eyes.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.840
But applying these kind of biological&nbsp;
inspiration on neural networks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.800
restricting their solution space&nbsp;
with biases is also unfair to them.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.000
It is important to take as much inspiration&nbsp;
from the biological optimization algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.720
especially when it comes to energy&nbsp;
efficiency, but we should also look beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:26.000
like with the Transformer that is free&nbsp;
of the LSTM and CNN inductive biases.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.480
Ok, so we have seen that images and&nbsp;
text can be represented the same and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.200
the Transformer heavily takes advantage of this.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.800
But if images and text are represented&nbsp;
equally, does that make them equal?

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.040
Well, no, images and text are&nbsp;
not born equal. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:49.520
they become very different content-wise:
Vision is often an evolutionary necessity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:55.280
and evolved independently in many species.&nbsp;
Language, human’s stroke of genius came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:58.800
later, after humans already had their vision.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.600
Being invented it is based&nbsp;
upon many agreed-upon rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.800
therefore text is in a way already&nbsp;
compressed; it omits a lot of stuff:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.320
we do not say that the grass is green,&nbsp;
if it is a (visual) common knowledge.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:20.880
Also, in the datasets Transfomers are usually&nbsp;
trained on, there is little if no figurative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.320
speech and there are very few ways to say&nbsp;
for example “cat” in English language.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:29.520
In contrast, images of cats can look very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.000
very different from each angle,&nbsp;
camera setting and so on.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:40.240
So omission of the visual information in language,&nbsp;
the necessary and somewhat arbitrary rules of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:46.080
language, cultures and languages (plural),
are few of the many things that make text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:52.400
and images quite different in content: There&nbsp;
is overlap, but they are also complementary.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.960
And Ms. Coffee Bean thinks that this is so&nbsp;
exciting that the representations and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.400
processing is similar, but the content and&nbsp;
nature of text and images are so different:

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.040
When the transformer acts on language, we&nbsp;
sometimes do not see the language phenomena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.600
because of so much transformer complexity
and the other times we do not see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.560
transformer because of so much language.
So it's the more helpful to learn something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:22.480
about transformers, text and images by seeing&nbsp;
them interact in different constellations.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.760
It's more or less like a&nbsp;
complicated ablation study.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.560
And the takeaway for multimodal&nbsp;
integration of all these three together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.840
is that even if text and images are&nbsp;
represented the same, they are not equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.880
in their content, giving Transformer&nbsp;
models a hard time to integrate them.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.400
The tendency is to kind of ignore the&nbsp;
visual information and rely on the text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:52.320
because text is already so compressed and&nbsp;
it's biases are more easy to be picked up.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.760
To find out more about this vision&nbsp;
and language inequality and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.240
effects on multimodal learning, check&nbsp;
out our video about this linked below.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.240
In any case, Ms. Coffee&nbsp;
Bean is out of here. Right?

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:04.636
Hey, Ms. Coffee Bean?

00:08:04.636 --> 00:08:05.520
Ms. Coffee Bean!

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.840
Ms. Coffee Bean, come!

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:11.840
Ok bye!

